// GAP (compile-clean IS the gap) — an element of a NON-NULL function-pointer
// array is NULL until assigned, and calling it is caught by nothing.
//
//   typedef u32 (*BinOp)(u32, u32);
//   BinOp[3] g_ops;          // accepted; auto-zero fills it with NULL
//   g_ops[0](1, 2);          // raw indirect call through address 0
//
// The scalar sibling is rejected (`BinOp g;` — "function pointer requires an
// initializer", BUG-866), so this is the same question answered differently for
// the array carrier. Measured 2026-08-25: the checker emits NO diagnostic.
//
// WHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG. Extending the non-null-requires-an-initializer
// rule through the array was implemented and REVERTED. ZER has no array
// initializer syntax, so the rule would not say "initialize it" — it would say
// "this type is unusable", removing the dispatch-table idiom entirely. It broke
// three corpus programs (tests/zer/funcptr_array.zer, callback_system.zer,
// func_pipeline.zer), all of which assign EVERY element before any use and are
// correct as written. That is a feature removal traded for a partial safety
// gain, which the ban-vs-track framework says not to do when a tracking system
// could cover it.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS ANYWAY. Hosted, the call faults and ZER's handler traps, so
// this is visible. On bare metal with no MMU, address 0 is the reset vector or
// ordinary memory: the jump silently goes somewhere, with no diagnostic at
// compile time OR run time. That is the silent-on-target class.
//
// FIX SKETCH, in preference order:
//  1. TRACK, do not ban: emit `if (!fp) _zer_trap(...)` before an indirect call
//     through a non-null funcptr that the compiler cannot prove assigned. Costs
//     one predictable branch, and GCC elides it wherever it can see the
//     assignment. Must be emitted at BOTH emitter dispatch paths (AST ~2525 and
//     the IR-rewritten one), and needs the callee hoisted to a temp for
//     single-evaluation — which is why it is not a one-liner and is not shipped
//     here rather than shipped hastily into the hottest emission path.
//  2. Definite-assignment analysis over array elements — the precise answer, and
//     a much larger change.
// The workaround available TODAY is the nullable element type, which is fully
// supported and pinned by tests/zer/funcptr_array_forms.zer:
//     ?BinOp[3] ops;   if (ops[i]) |f| { f(a, b); }
typedef u32 (*BinOp)(u32, u32);
u32 add(u32 a, u32 b) { return a + b; }
BinOp[3] g_ops;
u32 main() {
    g_ops[1] = add;
    return g_ops[0](1, 2);   // element 0 was never assigned
}
